Chapter: TlsEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter DtlsHello
  cites Encode chapter DtlsMessage
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Encode chapter TlsCert
  cites Foreword chapter Ed25519
  cites Foreword chapter DiffieHellman
  cites Foreword chapter Tls
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The TLS 1.3 endpoint over a reliable byte stream (RFC 8446): the thing
 that turns the record layer, the key schedule, the handshake messages and
 the X.509 authentication into a connection that can actually carry
 application data. It is the TCP sibling of DtlsEndpoint, and it reuses the
 pure RFC 8446 primitives that live -- for historical reasons -- in the
 Dtls* chapters: the transcript hash, the Finished, the ServerHello key
 share parser, the hello bodies. DTLS 1.3 inherits those from TLS 1.3
 unchanged, so this cites them rather than copying them.

 It is PURE: records in, records out, no socket and no clock. A whole
 authenticated handshake between a client and a server runs as an ordinary
 battery sample by shuttling the emitted records between two endpoints,
 exactly the way the DTLS loopback shuttles datagrams.

 What TLS-over-TCP adds over the DTLS endpoint, and why this is a separate
 chapter rather than a reuse of that one: the server flight after the
 ServerHello is ENCRYPTED under the handshake traffic keys (the DTLS
 endpoint sends it in the clear and says so), there is an
 EncryptedExtensions message, and the handshake derives the MASTER secret
 and the two APPLICATION traffic secrets, so this endpoint can carry
 application bytes. There is no cookie, no HelloRetryRequest-for-DoS and no
 retransmission: a reliable stream has none of the datagram problems those
 solve.

 AUTHENTICATION is Ed25519 X.509 only (RFC 8410), opt-in for the client via
 trust anchors. A client given anchors REFUSES to finish a handshake whose
 server did not present a chain that walks to one of them and sign the
 transcript -- which is what stops an active man in the middle. This
 authenticates a fleet whose CA you run; a commercial CA chain (RSA/ECDSA)
 cannot be validated, because the tree owns no such signature primitive.

 To tls-ep-hello gives TlsEpStep, starting a client and emitting its ClientHello.
 To tls-ep-recv gives TlsEpStep, feeding one record in and getting records out.
 To tls-ep-send-app gives TlsEpStep, encrypting one application record.

 We say:

Section: Types

  TlsEp = record {
    tep-server : Boolean,
    tep-priv : List Integer,
    tep-pub : List Integer,
    tep-random : List Integer,
    tep-transcript : List Integer,
    tep-hs-secret : List Integer,
    tep-c-hs : List Integer,
    tep-s-hs : List Integer,
    tep-c-ap : List Integer,
    tep-s-ap : List Integer,
    tep-read-seq : Integer,
    tep-write-seq : Integer,
    tep-app-read-seq : Integer,
    tep-app-write-seq : Integer,
    tep-phase : Integer,
    tep-cert-chain : List (List Integer),
    tep-sign-priv : List Integer,
    tep-sign-pub : List Integer,
    tep-anchors : List X509Anchor,
    tep-cal-now : List Integer,
    tep-peer-key : X509Key,
    tep-auth-done : Boolean,
    tep-expected-peer : List Integer,
    tep-done : Boolean,
    tep-app-in : List Integer
  }

  TlsEpStep = record {
    tes-ep : TlsEp,
    tes-out : List (List Integer)
  }

Section: Construction

 A client carries its X25519 private key, a 32-byte handshake random, the
 trust anchors it will accept, and the calendar time (14 ASCII digits) it
 checks certificate validity against. Entropy and clock enter from the
 caller and are never invented here.

  tls-ep-client-new : List Integer, List Integer, List X509Anchor, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tls-ep-client-new (priv) (random) (anchors) (cal-now) =
    TlsEp { tep-server = False, tep-priv = priv, tep-pub = x25519-public-key priv,
            tep-random = random, tep-transcript = [], tep-hs-secret = [], tep-c-hs = [], tep-s-hs = [],
            tep-c-ap = [], tep-s-ap = [], tep-read-seq = 0, tep-write-seq = 0, tep-app-read-seq = 0,
            tep-app-write-seq = 0, tep-phase = 0, tep-cert-chain = [], tep-sign-priv = [], tep-sign-pub = [],
            tep-anchors = anchors, tep-cal-now = cal-now,
            tep-peer-key = X509Key { xk-alg = KeyUnknown, xk-bytes = [] }, tep-auth-done = False,
            tep-expected-peer = [], tep-done = False, tep-app-in = [] }

 A server presents a certificate chain (leaf first) and the Ed25519 private
 key that matches the leaf, and signs the transcript with it.

  tls-ep-server-new : List Integer, List Integer, List (List Integer), List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tls-ep-server-new (priv) (random) (chain) (sign-priv) (sign-pub) =
    TlsEp { tep-server = True, tep-priv = priv, tep-pub = x25519-public-key priv,
            tep-random = random, tep-transcript = [], tep-hs-secret = [], tep-c-hs = [], tep-s-hs = [],
            tep-c-ap = [], tep-s-ap = [], tep-read-seq = 0, tep-write-seq = 0, tep-app-read-seq = 0,
            tep-app-write-seq = 0, tep-phase = 0, tep-cert-chain = chain, tep-sign-priv = sign-priv,
            tep-sign-pub = sign-pub, tep-anchors = [], tep-cal-now = [],
            tep-peer-key = X509Key { xk-alg = KeyUnknown, xk-bytes = [] }, tep-auth-done = False,
            tep-expected-peer = [], tep-done = False, tep-app-in = [] }

  tls-ep-expect-peer : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tls-ep-expect-peer (ep) (subject) = __record-set ep "tep-expected-peer" subject

Section: Record And Message Helpers

 The 32-byte hello random, taken from the caller's bytes.

  tep-rand32 : TlsEp -> List Integer
  tep-rand32 (ep) = dtls-take-at (ep.tep-random) 0 32

 Encrypt a payload as a TLS record under a traffic secret, deriving the
 AES-128-GCM key and IV from the secret each time.

  tep-enc : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  tep-enc (secret) (seq) (ctype) (payload) =
    tls13-record-encrypt (tls13-traffic-key secret) (tls13-traffic-iv secret) seq ctype payload

  tep-dec : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> Maybe TlsInner
  tep-dec (secret) (seq) (rec) =
    tls13-record-decrypt (tls13-traffic-key secret) (tls13-traffic-iv secret) seq rec

 The payload of a plaintext record (bytes past the 5-byte header, for the
 declared length).

  tep-rec-payload : List Integer -> List Integer
  tep-rec-payload (rec) = dtls-take-at rec 5 (list-at rec 3 * 256 + list-at rec 4)

 The body of a handshake message [type, u24(length), body].

  tep-msg-len : List Integer -> Integer
  tep-msg-len (m) = list-at m 1 * 65536 + list-at m 2 * 256 + list-at m 3

  tep-msg-body : List Integer -> List Integer
  tep-msg-body (m) = dtls-take-at m 4 (tep-msg-len m)

  tep-idle : TlsEp -> TlsEpStep
  tep-idle (ep) = TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep, tes-out = [] }

Section: Client Hello

  tls-ep-hello : TlsEp -> TlsEpStep
  tls-ep-hello (ep) =
    let body = dtls-client-hello-body (tep-rand32 ep) [] (ep.tep-pub) []
    in let msg = tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-client-hello body
    in let ep2 = __record-set (__record-set ep "tep-transcript" ((ep.tep-transcript) & msg)) "tep-phase" 1
    in TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep2, tes-out = [tls-encode-record tls-content-handshake msg] }

Section: Receive Dispatch

  tls-ep-recv : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tls-ep-recv (ep) (rec) =
    if list-length rec < 5 then tep-idle ep
    else let ct = list-at rec 0
    in if ct == tls-content-change-cipher then tep-idle ep
    else if ep.tep-server then tep-server-recv ep rec ct
    else tep-client-recv ep rec ct

Section: Client Receive

  tep-client-recv : TlsEp, List Integer, Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-recv (ep) (rec) (ct) =
    if ct == tls-content-handshake then tep-client-sh ep (tep-rec-payload rec)
    else if ct == tls-content-application then
      (if ep.tep-phase >= 3 then tep-client-app ep rec else tep-client-enc-hs ep rec)
    else tep-idle ep

 The ServerHello arrives in the clear. Parse the server's key share, do the
 X25519 agreement, and derive the handshake traffic secrets over the
 transcript through the ServerHello.

  tep-client-sh : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-sh (ep) (msg) =
    when dtls-sh-key-share (tep-msg-body msg)
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (peer) -> tep-client-derive ep (tep-msg-body msg) peer

  tep-client-derive : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-derive (ep) (sh-body) (peer) =
    let tr = (ep.tep-transcript) & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-server-hello sh-body
    in let hs = tls13-handshake-secret (x25519-shared-secret (ep.tep-priv) peer)
    in let th = tls13-transcript-hash tr
    in let ep2 = tep-store-hs ep tr hs (tls13-client-hs-traffic hs th) (tls13-server-hs-traffic hs th)
    in TlsEpStep { tes-ep = __record-set (__record-set ep2 "tep-phase" 2) "tep-read-seq" 0, tes-out = [] }

  tep-store-hs : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tep-store-hs (ep) (tr) (hs) (c) (s) =
    __record-set (__record-set (__record-set (__record-set ep "tep-transcript" tr) "tep-hs-secret" hs) "tep-c-hs" c) "tep-s-hs" s

 An encrypted handshake record. Decrypt under the server handshake key at
 the current read sequence, bump the sequence for every record in the
 epoch, and fold every handshake message the record carried into the
 machine -- servers are allowed to coalesce EncryptedExtensions,
 Certificate, CertificateVerify and Finished into one record.

  tep-client-enc-hs : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-enc-hs (ep) (rec) =
    when tep-dec (ep.tep-s-hs) (ep.tep-read-seq) rec
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (inner) ->
        (if inner.ti-type /= tls-content-handshake then __step-bump-read ep
         else tep-client-hs-msgs (__record-set ep "tep-read-seq" (ep.tep-read-seq + 1)) (inner.ti-data) 0 [])

  __step-bump-read : TlsEp -> TlsEpStep
  __step-bump-read (ep) = TlsEpStep { tes-ep = __record-set ep "tep-read-seq" (ep.tep-read-seq + 1), tes-out = [] }

  tep-client-hs-msgs : TlsEp, List Integer, Integer, List (List Integer) -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-hs-msgs (ep) (buf) (off) (outs) =
    if off + 4 > list-length buf then TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep, tes-out = outs }
    else let len = list-at buf (off + 1) * 65536 + list-at buf (off + 2) * 256 + list-at buf (off + 3)
    in if off + 4 + len > list-length buf then TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep, tes-out = outs }
    else let step = tep-client-hs-one ep (list-at buf off) (dtls-take-at buf (off + 4) len)
    in tep-client-hs-msgs (step.tes-ep) buf (off + 4 + len) (outs & step.tes-out)

  tep-client-hs-one : TlsEp, Integer, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-hs-one (ep) (t) (body) =
    if t == dtls-hs-encrypted-ext then tep-fold ep t body
    else if t == dtls-hs-certificate then tep-client-cert ep body
    else if t == dtls-hs-cert-verify then tep-client-cv ep body
    else if t == dtls-hs-finished then tep-client-fin ep body
    else tep-idle ep

  tep-fold : TlsEp, Integer, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-fold (ep) (t) (body) =
    TlsEpStep { tes-ep = __record-set ep "tep-transcript" ((ep.tep-transcript) & tls13-msg-transcript t body), tes-out = [] }

 The Certificate message: parse the chain, walk it to an anchor at the
 client's calendar time, and check the leaf's subject against the pinned
 peer if one is set. On success keep the leaf's key.

 THE TRANSCRIPT IS A RECORD OF WHAT WAS RECEIVED, NOT OF WHAT WAS
 BELIEVED. Both this message and CertificateVerify fold unconditionally.
 They used to fold only on success, which cost a whole capability: a
 client with NO trust anchors does not require authentication
 (tep-auth-missing says so), but it also cannot verify a chain, so the
 Certificate went unfolded, the client's transcript diverged from the
 server's by exactly that message, and the server Finished then failed to
 verify. An unauthenticated handshake was impossible -- not because we
 refused it, but because declining to record a message we could not check
 corrupted the hash. Nothing in the loopback could see it: there the
 certificate always verifies.

 The security property is unmoved and lives one layer up. Verification
 decides tep-peer-key and tep-auth-done, and tep-auth-missing refuses the
 Finished when anchors were supplied and authentication did not happen. A
 folded message is not a trusted one.

  tep-client-cert : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-cert (ep) (body) =
    tep-fold (tep-cert-peer-key ep body) dtls-hs-certificate body

  tep-cert-peer-key : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tep-cert-peer-key (ep) (body) =
    when tls-cert-parse body
      is None -> ep
      is Just (ders) -> tep-cert-key-chain ep ders

  tep-cert-key-chain : TlsEp, List (List Integer) -> TlsEp
  tep-cert-key-chain (ep) (ders) =
    when tep-parse-chain ders 0 []
      is None -> ep
      is Just (certs) -> tep-cert-key-walk ep certs

  tep-cert-key-walk : TlsEp, List X509Cert -> TlsEp
  tep-cert-key-walk (ep) (certs) =
    when x509-chain-verify certs (ep.tep-anchors) (ep.tep-cal-now)
      is ChainOk ->
        (if tep-peer-matches ep (list-at certs 0)
         then __record-set ep "tep-peer-key" ((list-at certs 0).xc-key)
         else ep)
      is otherwise -> ep

  tep-peer-matches : TlsEp, X509Cert -> Boolean
  tep-peer-matches (ep) (leaf) =
    if list-length (ep.tep-expected-peer) == 0 then True
    else x509-name-eq (ep.tep-expected-peer) (leaf.xc-subject)

 CertificateVerify: the signature is checked over the transcript hash
 THROUGH the Certificate -- the transcript as it stands before this
 message is folded in. The verdict becomes tep-auth-done; the message
 folds either way.

  tep-client-cv : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-cv (ep) (body) =
    let ok = tls-cv-verify True (ep.tep-peer-key) body (tls13-transcript-hash (ep.tep-transcript))
    in tep-fold (__record-set ep "tep-auth-done" ok) dtls-hs-cert-verify body

 The server's Finished. A client that required authentication and did not
 get it stops here. Otherwise verify the Finished over the transcript
 through CertificateVerify, then fold it, derive the application secrets
 over the transcript through the server Finished, and send our own Finished
 -- encrypted under the client handshake key -- over that same hash.

  tep-client-fin : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-fin (ep) (body) =
    if tep-auth-missing ep then tep-idle ep
    else if tls13-finished-ok (ep.tep-s-hs) (tls13-transcript-hash (ep.tep-transcript)) body == False then tep-idle ep
    else tep-client-finish ep body

  tep-auth-missing : TlsEp -> Boolean
  tep-auth-missing (ep) =
    if list-length (ep.tep-anchors) == 0 then False else ep.tep-auth-done == False

  tep-client-finish : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-finish (ep) (sfin) =
    let tr = (ep.tep-transcript) & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-finished sfin
    in let th = tls13-transcript-hash tr
    in let master = tls13-master-secret (ep.tep-hs-secret)
    in let cfin = tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-finished (tls13-verify-data (ep.tep-c-hs) th)
    in let rec = tep-enc (ep.tep-c-hs) (ep.tep-write-seq) tls-content-handshake cfin
    in let ep2 = tep-store-app ep tr (tls13-client-ap-traffic master th) (tls13-server-ap-traffic master th)
    in TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep2, tes-out = [rec] }

  tep-store-app : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tep-store-app (ep) (tr) (c-ap) (s-ap) =
    let e1 = __record-set (__record-set (__record-set ep "tep-transcript" tr) "tep-c-ap" c-ap) "tep-s-ap" s-ap
    in let e2 = __record-set (__record-set (__record-set e1 "tep-phase" 3) "tep-app-read-seq" 0) "tep-app-write-seq" 0
    in __record-set (__record-set e2 "tep-done" True) "tep-app-in" (__list-with-capacity tep-app-cap)

  tep-client-app : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-client-app (ep) (rec) =
    when tep-dec (ep.tep-s-ap) (ep.tep-app-read-seq) rec
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (inner) -> TlsEpStep { tes-ep = tep-app-accept ep inner, tes-out = [] }

 A record in the application epoch is only application DATA if its inner
 content type says so. A server's post-handshake NewSessionTicket or a
 KeyUpdate arrives here as an inner handshake record; it advances the
 sequence like any other record but must never be appended to the response
 body, or the body is corrupted by bytes the caller never asked for.

  tep-app-cap : Integer = 262144

  tep-app-accept : TlsEp, TlsInner -> TlsEp
  tep-app-accept (ep) (inner) =
    let e1 = __record-set ep "tep-app-read-seq" (ep.tep-app-read-seq + 1)
    in if inner.ti-type == tls-content-application
    then __record-set e1 "tep-app-in" (tep-append-bytes (ep.tep-app-in) (inner.ti-data) 0 (list-length (inner.ti-data)))
    else e1

 Application data accumulates into ONE list, reserved to capacity at the
 handshake's end and pushed into in place -- the same discipline http-recv
 uses on the cleartext path. The obvious `app-in & chunk` per record copies
 the whole buffer every record, which is quadratic in the number of records
 and, on a heap with no collector, leaves every intermediate copy live until
 the fetch returns. list-push writes in place while the list is under
 capacity, so the accumulation is linear; a body past the cap stops growing
 rather than reallocating.

  tep-append-bytes : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  tep-append-bytes (dst) (src) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then dst
    else if list-length dst >= tep-app-cap then dst
    else tep-append-bytes (list-push dst (list-at src i)) src (i + 1) n

Section: Chain Parse

  tep-parse-chain : List (List Integer), Integer, List X509Cert -> Maybe (List X509Cert)
  tep-parse-chain (ders) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ders then Just acc
    else tep-parse-one ders i acc

  tep-parse-one : List (List Integer), Integer, List X509Cert -> Maybe (List X509Cert)
  tep-parse-one (ders) (i) (acc) =
    when x509-parse (list-at ders i)
      is None -> None
      is Just (c) -> tep-parse-chain ders (i + 1) (list-push acc c)

Section: Server Receive

  tep-server-recv : TlsEp, List Integer, Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-recv (ep) (rec) (ct) =
    if ct == tls-content-handshake then tep-server-ch ep (tep-rec-payload rec)
    else if ct == tls-content-application then
      (if ep.tep-phase >= 3 then tep-server-app ep rec else tep-server-enc-hs ep rec)
    else tep-idle ep

  tep-server-ch : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-ch (ep) (msg) =
    when dtls-ch-key-share (tep-msg-body msg)
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (peer) -> tep-server-flight ep (tep-msg-body msg) peer

 The whole server flight in one place: ServerHello in the clear, then
 EncryptedExtensions, Certificate, CertificateVerify and Finished each in
 its own record under the server handshake key. The application secrets are
 derived over the transcript through the server Finished -- the same hash
 the client's Finished will be checked against.

  tep-server-flight : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-flight (ep) (ch-body) (peer) =
    let tr0 = (ep.tep-transcript) & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-client-hello ch-body
    in let sh-body = dtls-server-hello-body (tep-rand32 ep) (dtls-ch-session-id ch-body) (ep.tep-pub)
    in let tr1 = tr0 & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-server-hello sh-body
    in let hs = tls13-handshake-secret (x25519-shared-secret (ep.tep-priv) peer)
    in let th1 = tls13-transcript-hash tr1
    in let c-hs = tls13-client-hs-traffic hs th1
    in let s-hs = tls13-server-hs-traffic hs th1
    in let ee-body = [0, 0]
    in let tr2 = tr1 & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-encrypted-ext ee-body
    in let cert-body = tls-cert-encode (ep.tep-cert-chain)
    in let tr3 = tr2 & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-certificate cert-body
    in let cv-body = tls-cv-encode True (ep.tep-sign-priv) (ep.tep-sign-pub) (tls13-transcript-hash tr3)
    in let tr4 = tr3 & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-cert-verify cv-body
    in let sfin = tls13-verify-data s-hs (tls13-transcript-hash tr4)
    in let tr5 = tr4 & tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-finished sfin
    in let th5 = tls13-transcript-hash tr5
    in let master = tls13-master-secret hs
    in tep-server-emit ep tr5 hs c-hs s-hs (tls13-client-ap-traffic master th5) (tls13-server-ap-traffic master th5) sh-body ee-body cert-body cv-body sfin

  tep-server-emit : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-emit (ep) (tr5) (hs) (c-hs) (s-hs) (c-ap) (s-ap) (sh-body) (ee-body) (cert-body) (cv-body) (sfin) =
    let sh-rec = tls-encode-record tls-content-handshake (tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-server-hello sh-body)
    in let ee-rec = tep-enc s-hs 0 tls-content-handshake (tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-encrypted-ext ee-body)
    in let cert-rec = tep-enc s-hs 1 tls-content-handshake (tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-certificate cert-body)
    in let cv-rec = tep-enc s-hs 2 tls-content-handshake (tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-cert-verify cv-body)
    in let fin-rec = tep-enc s-hs 3 tls-content-handshake (tls13-msg-transcript dtls-hs-finished sfin)
    in let ep2 = tep-server-state ep tr5 hs c-hs s-hs c-ap s-ap
    in TlsEpStep { tes-ep = ep2, tes-out = [sh-rec, ee-rec, cert-rec, cv-rec, fin-rec] }

  tep-server-state : TlsEp, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> TlsEp
  tep-server-state (ep) (tr) (hs) (c-hs) (s-hs) (c-ap) (s-ap) =
    let e1 = tep-store-hs ep tr hs c-hs s-hs
    in let e2 = __record-set (__record-set e1 "tep-c-ap" c-ap) "tep-s-ap" s-ap
    in let e3 = __record-set (__record-set (__record-set e2 "tep-phase" 2) "tep-read-seq" 0) "tep-app-read-seq" 0
    in __record-set e3 "tep-app-in" (__list-with-capacity tep-app-cap)

 The client's Finished, encrypted under the client handshake key. Verify it
 over the transcript through the server Finished; if it holds, both sides
 share the handshake secret and the connection is open.

  tep-server-enc-hs : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-enc-hs (ep) (rec) =
    when tep-dec (ep.tep-c-hs) (ep.tep-read-seq) rec
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (inner) ->
        (if inner.ti-type /= tls-content-handshake then tep-idle ep else tep-server-fin ep (inner.ti-data))

  tep-server-fin : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-fin (ep) (buf) =
    let vd = dtls-take-at buf 4 (list-at buf 1 * 65536 + list-at buf 2 * 256 + list-at buf 3)
    in if tls13-finished-ok (ep.tep-c-hs) (tls13-transcript-hash (ep.tep-transcript)) vd == False then tep-idle ep
    else TlsEpStep { tes-ep = __record-set (__record-set (__record-set ep "tep-phase" 3) "tep-app-write-seq" 0) "tep-done" True, tes-out = [] }

  tep-server-app : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tep-server-app (ep) (rec) =
    when tep-dec (ep.tep-c-ap) (ep.tep-app-read-seq) rec
      is None -> tep-idle ep
      is Just (inner) -> TlsEpStep { tes-ep = tep-app-accept ep inner, tes-out = [] }

Section: Application Data

  tls-ep-send-app : TlsEp, List Integer -> TlsEpStep
  tls-ep-send-app (ep) (data) =
    let sec = if ep.tep-server then ep.tep-s-ap else ep.tep-c-ap
    in let rec = tep-enc sec (ep.tep-app-write-seq) tls-content-application data
    in TlsEpStep { tes-ep = __record-set ep "tep-app-write-seq" (ep.tep-app-write-seq + 1), tes-out = [rec] }

Section: Accessors

  tls-ep-done : TlsEp -> Boolean
  tls-ep-done (ep) = ep.tep-done

  tls-ep-authenticated : TlsEp -> Boolean
  tls-ep-authenticated (ep) = ep.tep-auth-done

  tls-ep-app-in : TlsEp -> List Integer
  tls-ep-app-in (ep) = ep.tep-app-in
